<%!-- The impersonal public guide (#817): what the agent setup does,
no prompt and no client snippets — those render only on the signed-in
surfaces, the `/agent` panel and the dashboard's first-run modal. The
docs reading surface (ADR-0044 §5) — sidebar left, article column
centered. --%>
<Layouts.public flash={@flash} current_user={@current_user} return_to={@return_to}>
  <div class="docs docs-with-nav">
    <FluxValeWeb.DocsHTML.sidebar sections={@sections} active_agent_docs={true} />

    <article class="docs-main">
      <header class="docs-article-head">
        <p class="docs-crumb">
          <a href={~p"/docs"}>Docs</a>
          <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
          <span>Agent setup</span>
        </p>
        <h1 class="docs-title text-balance">Set up with your agent</h1>
        <p class="docs-standfirst">
          One copyable prompt drives an agent through a FluxVale first run over
          the MCP server: check the wallet, propose an app with its cost, deploy
          it with your env vars, and hand back the URL. Claude Code, Cursor,
          VS Code, or any MCP client speaks it. This page explains the setup —
          the personalized prompt lives behind sign-in.
        </p>
      </header>

      <div class="docs-body md-body">
        <p :if={@current_user}>
          Signed in?
          <a href={~p"/agent"} data-testid="agent-docs-personalized-link">Open your personalized panel</a>
        </p>
        <p :if={is_nil(@current_user)}>
          Have an account?
          <a href={@sign_in_path} data-testid="agent-docs-sign-in-link">Sign in to get your prompt</a>
          — you land back on the personalized panel.
        </p>

        <h2>What the agent does</h2>
        <p>
          The prompt names your org, the API base, and the MCP server, then
          walks the agent through the first run in order:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>Check the wallet; at zero, stop and hand back the top-up link.</li>
          <li>Pick an app from the catalog and propose one version with its price.</li>
          <li>
            Ask once whether you want single sign-on (OIDC) for your apps. Say no and it
            moves on without asking again. Say yes and it explains that SSO is a paid-org
            feature, recommends a top-up ({FluxValeWeb.BillingComponents.topup_ladder_choices()},
            or above {FluxValeWeb.BillingComponents.topup_ladder_top()}), waits while you
            check out in your browser, and then deploys and wires Pocket ID.
          </li>
          <li>Confirm the spend with you before deploying.</li>
          <li>Deploy with your env vars and poll until the instance is running.</li>
          <li>Hand back the public instance URL; env changes come later, write-only.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          <strong>It asks before it spends.</strong> Destructive tools —
          instance destroy, backup restore — need a separate, explicit yes.
        </p>

        <h2>Get the prompt</h2>
        <p>
          The personalized prompt renders after sign-in: on the agent panel,
          and in the onboarding modal your empty dashboard opens on first run.
          The per-client install snippets — Claude Code, Cursor, VS Code,
          generic JSON — ride along there too.
        </p>
        <p>
          If your client runs on a machine without a browser (over SSH, in a
          container), the FluxVale CLI bridges it to the server. The
          <a href={~p"/docs/automation/cli"}>CLI guide</a>
          has the two commands.
        </p>

        <h2>Keep reading</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            The <a href={~p"/docs/automation/mcp"}>MCP server guide</a>
            lists every tool and its scope.
          </li>
          <li>
            The <a href={~p"/docs/automation/terraform"}>Terraform provider</a>
            is the option to offer once an app is running.
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href={~p"/llms.txt"}>/llms.txt</a> carries the generic prompt for agents.
          </li>
          <li>
            The <a href={~p"/docs/guides/sso"}>Pocket ID SSO guide</a>
            covers passkey sign-in and per-app OIDC wiring.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</Layouts.public>
